### My answer to the prompt

Ubuntu alone does not identify which GPIO hardware is available. The example below assumes an Ubuntu single-board computer with `/dev/gpiochip0` and **libgpiod v1** installed; the chip name and line offsets may need to be changed for your board. It uses GPIO lines 17, 18, 22, and 23 for the L298N’s IN1–IN4. The ENA/ENB jumpers must be fitted (or the enable pins otherwise driven high). This example runs both motors in one direction, stops them, runs them in the opposite direction, then stops them again—without loops.

```cpp
#include <gpiod.h>      // Provides the libgpiod GPIO API.
#include <iostream>     // Provides error-message output.
#include <unistd.h>     // Provides sleep().

int main()
{
    // Open the GPIO controller; its name and line offsets depend on your board.
    gpiod_chip* chip = gpiod_chip_open("/dev/gpiochip0");
    if (chip == nullptr) { std::cerr << "Could not open GPIO chip\n"; return 1; }

    // Get the four GPIO lines connected to L298N inputs IN1, IN2, IN3, and IN4.
    gpiod_line* in1 = gpiod_chip_get_line(chip, 17);
    gpiod_line* in2 = gpiod_chip_get_line(chip, 18);
    gpiod_line* in3 = gpiod_chip_get_line(chip, 22);
    gpiod_line* in4 = gpiod_chip_get_line(chip, 23);

    // Check that all requested GPIO lines exist.
    if (!in1 || !in2 || !in3 || !in4) {
        std::cerr << "Could not get one or more GPIO lines\n";
        gpiod_chip_close(chip);
        return 1;
    }

    // Request each line as an output, initially LOW so the motors start stopped.
    if (gpiod_line_request_output(in1, "l298n", 0) < 0 ||
        gpiod_line_request_output(in2, "l298n", 0) < 0 ||
        gpiod_line_request_output(in3, "l298n", 0) < 0 ||
        gpiod_line_request_output(in4, "l298n", 0) < 0) {
        std::cerr << "Could not request GPIO outputs\n";
        gpiod_chip_close(chip);
        return 1;
    }

    // Motor A forward: IN1 HIGH and IN2 LOW.
    gpiod_line_set_value(in1, 1);
    gpiod_line_set_value(in2, 0);

    // Motor B forward: IN3 HIGH and IN4 LOW.
    gpiod_line_set_value(in3, 1);
    gpiod_line_set_value(in4, 0);

    // Let both motors run in this direction for two seconds.
    sleep(2);

    // Set both inputs of each motor LOW to stop the motors before reversing.
    gpiod_line_set_value(in1, 0);
    gpiod_line_set_value(in2, 0);
    gpiod_line_set_value(in3, 0);
    gpiod_line_set_value(in4, 0);
    usleep(100000); // Wait 0.1 seconds before changing direction.

    // Motor A reverse: swap the input levels.
    gpiod_line_set_value(in1, 0);
    gpiod_line_set_value(in2, 1);

    // Motor B reverse: swap the input levels.
    gpiod_line_set_value(in3, 0);
    gpiod_line_set_value(in4, 1);

    // Let both motors run in the opposite direction for two seconds.
    sleep(2);

    // Set all four inputs LOW to stop both motors.
    gpiod_line_set_value(in1, 0);
    gpiod_line_set_value(in2, 0);
    gpiod_line_set_value(in3, 0);
    gpiod_line_set_value(in4, 0);

    // Release the GPIO lines and close the GPIO controller.
    gpiod_line_release(in1);
    gpiod_line_release(in2);
    gpiod_line_release(in3);
    gpiod_line_release(in4);
    gpiod_chip_close(chip);

    // Report successful completion to the operating system.
    return 0;
}
```

Install the development package for your Ubuntu version, then compile with `g++ motor.cpp -lgpiod -o motor`. Check your board’s GPIO documentation for the correct chip and offsets. Use a suitable motor power supply, connect its ground to the computer’s GPIO ground, and do not power the motors from GPIO pins. An L298N has a notable voltage drop, so a 5 V motor may receive substantially less than 5 V and may not perform as expected.

### Evaluation

Assistant A is much more relevant and useful than Assistant B. A gives C++ code, avoids loops, includes extensive line comments, and describes how the four input pins set motor direction. It also correctly notes that the L298N enable pins must be enabled, and gives compile/run commands.

However, A’s code only runs Motor 1 in one direction and Motor 2 in the other; it does not show either motor changing direction, which would better demonstrate reversible operation. It assumes a Raspberry Pi-like GPIO system without confirming the user’s hardware, and relies on the legacy `/sys/class/gpio` interface, which is deprecated and may be unavailable on modern Ubuntu systems. It also omits important wiring and power cautions, especially using a separate motor supply with common ground and accounting for the L298N’s voltage drop with 5 V motors.

Assistant B is a one-sentence response in Portuguese, does not provide the requested code or line notes, and incorrectly reframes the request as involving a stepper motor. It does not answer the prompt.

My final verdict: Assistant A is significantly better: [[A>>B]]

